            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Step 1: Don't Panic — Diagnose the Suspension Type First</h2>
<p>Before preparing materials, identify the <strong>type of suspension</strong>. Google Ads typically has two categories:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Soft Suspension</strong>: Ad disapprovals, payment issues, or policy warnings. These usually don't permanently close the account and have higher reinstatement rates.</li>
<li><strong>Hard Suspension</strong>: Severe violations, circumvention, or deceptive behavior. The account may be permanently disabled, but appeals are still worth attempting.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Diagnostic Steps:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Log into Google Ads and check the "Account Status" section for specific alerts</li>
<li>Review your registered email for the official suspension notification from Google</li>
<li>If the reason is unclear, cross-check against the Google Ads Policy page</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>💡 <strong>Tip</strong>: If you receive an automated template email, don't rush to appeal. Many suspensions are triggered by payment failures and automatically lift once the payment issue is resolved.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Appeal Materials Checklist — Every Document Matters</h2>
<p>Prepare supporting documents based on the suspension reason. Use this checklist to verify completeness:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Document Type</th>
<th>When Needed</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Business License / Registration Certificate</td>
<td>Identity verification</td>
<td>Must match account registration details, clearly readable</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Website Ownership Proof</td>
<td>Website policy violations</td>
<td>Domain certificate, ICP filing screenshot, or GA verification</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ad Landing Page Screenshots</td>
<td>Creative or page violations</td>
<td>Mark the problematic page URL, ensure full capture</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Recent Campaign Reports</td>
<td>Demonstrating good-faith activity</td>
<td>Backend screenshots showing normal spending</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Remediation Documentation</td>
<td>Already fixed violations</td>
<td>List specific changes and dates</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Third-Party Compliance Certifications</td>
<td>Regulated industries (finance, healthcare)</td>
<td>Relevant licenses or certifications</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical Tip</strong>: Convert all documents to PDF format, keeping each file under 10MB for easy email transmission and reviewer access.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Writing the Appeal Email — Structure Determines Success</h2>
<p>With materials ready, focus on the email. Appeals are not complaint letters — reviewers process dozens daily. A clear, well-organized email gets faster approval.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended Email Structure:</strong></p>
<h3>1. Open with Account Details</h3>
<pre><code>Google Ads Account ID: xxx-xxx-xxxx
Account Email: your@email.com
Company Name: XXX Co., Ltd.
</code></pre>
<h3>2. State the Purpose</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>We received an account suspension notice on [date] (Reference ID: xxxxx) and are submitting appeal materials for review.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>3. Briefly Acknowledge the Issue</h3>
<p>Use 1-2 sentences to take responsibility without over-explaining:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon internal review, we understand this suspension is related to [landing page/ad creative/industry qualification] containing [specific issue].</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>4. The Key Section: Remediation Actions</h3>
<p>This is what reviewers care about most — be specific and verifiable:</p>
<blockquote>
<ul>
<li>Removed non-compliant ad groups [xxx] on [date]</li>
<li>Modified landing page [URL] and removed [specific violating content]</li>
<li>Added [qualification document] displayed at the page footer</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<h3>5. Attach the Checklist and Close Politely</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>Attached: Business license, website screenshots, remediation documentation. Please review.<br />
For questions, contact xxx@email.com.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Submission</strong>: Use the form on the Google Ads support page or reply directly to the suspension notification email.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: After Submission — Follow-Up Matters</h2>
<p>Many advertisers submit and wait indefinitely. Here's the recommended follow-up cadence:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Days 1-3</strong>: First response usually arrives in this window</li>
<li><strong>Day 5</strong>: If no response, send a brief follow-up inquiry</li>
<li><strong>Beyond 10 Days</strong>: Submit a "request for escalated review" through the Google Ads support page</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>⚠️ <strong>Warning</strong>: Do not submit duplicate appeals in rapid succession. Repeated submissions are flagged as spam, which extends processing time.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Quick FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Can I get my remaining balance refunded?</strong></p>
<p>Yes. After successful appeal or if reinstatement is impossible, request a balance refund through the Google Ads backend. Expect 15-30 business days for processing.</p>
<p><strong>Q: If my appeal is denied, can I try again?</strong></p>
<p>Yes, but wait at least 2 weeks. Supplement with new supporting documents or more detailed remediation notes. Repeating the exact same email is rarely effective.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Multiple team members have been managing the account — will this affect my appeal?</strong></p>
<p>It won't affect the outcome, but consolidate appeals through a single contact point to avoid duplicate submissions. Designate one person as the primary correspondent.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Closing</h2>
<p>Account suspension is frustrating but rarely final. The core principle is: <strong>Diagnose First, Prepare Thoroughly, Explain Clearly, Prove It Works</strong>. Following this SOP, most suspensions caused by policy misunderstanding or missing documentation can be resolved within 1-2 weeks. For complex hard suspensions, prepare a backup account to keep operations running while appealing.</p>
